  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.4 x² + 0.4 z²) / 2, steep left-right, gentle front-back. The surface is drawn at half the loss height. Plain descent multiplies x by 1 − 2.4 × rate each step, so swings grow (diverge) above a rate of 0.83.",
    "Each step = −rate × (true slope + noise). Each noise component has spread sqrt((64 − b) / (b × 63)) for batch b: 1 at b = 1, exactly 0 at b = 64. Because the noise stays the same near the bottom, small batches keep wandering there.",
    "Both arrows show steps (rate × slope estimate), not the slope itself. They are drawn 6 times their true length, the same scale for both, so at large rates they get long. The step-length readout is the true length.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished) or when the loss passes 5 (diverged, the ball has left the bowl). Off-bowl path points are drawn at the rim. Noise comes from a fixed seed, so Reset replays the same noise from the start.",
    "A running scene takes 5 steps per second at speed 1. New run, keep last path starts again from the same point with your current settings, keeps the last path as the ghost and uses the next noise values."
  ]
}